  "pd": {
    "basis": "author-died-pre-1930",
    "detail": "Johann Sebastian Bach died in 1750; Book I of Das Wohltemperierte Klavier was completed in 1722 and first printed in 1801. The work is free worldwide.",
    "sourceEdition": "Bach, Das Wohltemperierte Klavier Book I, BWV 846, Bach-Gesellschaft Ausgabe (Breitkopf und Haertel, Leipzig, 1866), vol. 14, Prelude No. 1",
    "notes": "Melody and harmony only; the prelude is wordless keyboard music. Written in common time and encoded as 4/4 with no tempo remapping. The piece is one continuous broken-chord figuration, so the \"melody\" is that figuration written out as a single sixteenth-note line: each bar states its five-note chord as b1 b2 r1 r2 r3 r1 r2 r3 and repeats it for the second half bar, exactly as the score lays it out. Encoded one octave above written pitch to keep the line in a usable lead register. Bars 1-19 are carried; the long dominant/tonic pedal coda (bars 20-35) is not. The final bar of the Cadence section is an editorial close: the bar-19 figuration restated for half a bar over a held tonic, so the excerpt ends instead of breaking off. Verified bar by bar on a second pass; bar 13 was corrected to F A D A D, the first-inversion voicing (left hand on the third and fifth, right hand root-fifth-root) that bars 5 and 7 also use, which is what lets the C# of bar 12 resolve up to D. Confidence is medium because that bar was reconstructed from the voicing pattern and the voice leading rather than read off the page."
  },
